<h2>An Identity Crisis Unfolding Today</h2>

<p>In 2026, PwC released its annual Digital Trends report with the subtitle &quot;How AI Reinvents Enterprise Performance.&quot; Deloitte published its annual &quot;State of AI in the Enterprise&quot; report. MIT Sloan Review explored &quot;How to boost your organization's AI maturity level.&quot; Workday released &quot;What Are the Biggest HR Challenges of 2026.&quot;</p>

<p>These seemingly independent reports all point to the same issue: <strong>AI is systematically reshaping the role of &quot;manager.&quot;</strong> But a deeper question emerges: when AI can do more and more of what was traditionally considered &quot;management work,&quot; where exactly does a leader's value lie?</p>

<h2>Insight 1: From &quot;Decision-Maker&quot; to &quot;Human-AI Collaboration Designer&quot;</h2>

<p>Traditional managers have three core functions: information processing, decision-making, and coordination/control. AI is challenging managers on all three fronts.</p>

<p>PwC's 2026 Digital Trends report notes that AI is no longer just an efficiency tool — it is redefining how enterprises operate. This means the manager's role is shifting from &quot;I say, you do&quot; to <strong>&quot;I design how humans and AI work together.&quot;</strong></p>

<p>This shift is critical: managers no longer need to make every decision, but they do need to <strong>decide which decisions go to AI, which stay with humans, and how the handoff works between them.</strong> This is essentially the organizational practice dimension of the Human-AI Fit concept in Connie's research.</p>

<h2>Insight 2: AI Maturity Becomes the New Measure of Leadership</h2>

<p>Deloitte's 2026 AI report surveyed thousands of enterprises globally and found that AI maturity is no longer a KPI for the IT department — it is a <strong>strategic agenda that the CEO must personally drive</strong>.</p>

<p>Organizations with high AI maturity share a common trait among their leaders: <strong>dual competence in AI literacy and humanistic vision.</strong> You don't need to write code, but you do need to understand what AI can and cannot do, and how to help your team adopt AI-driven workflow changes with minimal friction.</p>

<p>MIT Sloan Review's article &quot;How to boost your organization's AI maturity level&quot; offers a key insight: the bottleneck for AI adoption is not technology, but leadership — specifically, <strong>leaders' lack of organizational change management capability in the AI era</strong>.</p>

<h2>Insight 3: Middle Managers' &quot;Displacement Anxiety&quot; Is Real</h2>

<p>Workday's 2026 HR Challenges report carries an implicit message: <strong>middle managers are experiencing the most profound identity crisis.</strong></p>

<p>The reason is sobering: when AI can handle — schedule coordination, report writing, data analysis, basic performance evaluation — tasks that traditionally occupied 60-70% of middle managers' time, the role of middle manager needs to be redefined.</p>

<p>This could lead to two outcomes:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Positive</strong>: Middle managers are freed from routine tasks, pivoting toward more valuable strategic thinking and team empowerment</li>
  <li><strong>Negative</strong>: Middle managers fall into &quot;defensive turf protection,&quot; becoming a barrier to AI transformation</li>
</ul>

<p>The key variable? <strong>Leaders' cognition and behavior</strong> — which is precisely the core determinant of an organization's AI maturity.</p>

<h2>Insight 4: The More Powerful AI Gets, the More Important &quot;Soft Skills&quot; Become</h2>

<p>This may be the most counterintuitive conclusion. The logic goes like this:</p>

<ul>
  <li>When AI can handle <strong>information</strong>, human value shifts to <strong>meaning</strong></li>
  <li>When AI can provide <strong>answers</strong>, human value shifts to <strong>questions</strong></li>
  <li>When AI can complete <strong>tasks</strong>, human value shifts to <strong>judgment</strong></li>
</ul>

<p>HBR's April 2026 study (&quot;Why Companies That Choose AI Augmentation Over Automation May Win in the Long Run&quot;) provides empirical support for this view. It found that companies choosing &quot;AI augmentation&quot; over &quot;AI automation&quot; perform better in long-term competitiveness.</p>

<p>This means, <strong>in the AI era, empathy, cultural understanding, and ethical judgment — skills once considered &quot;nice-to-have&quot; — are becoming core competitive strengths.</strong></p>

<h2>Connections to Connie's Research Areas</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Human-AI Fit</strong>: <strong>Core relevance</strong> — This article addresses the central question of &quot;what is the unique value of human leaders in the AI era,&quot; which is the organizational dimension of Human-AI Fit research</li>
  <li><strong>Knowledge Management</strong>: Improving AI maturity requires a supporting knowledge management system — AI can generate information, but only humans can turn it into organizational wisdom</li>
  <li><strong>Globalization</strong>: AI leadership in multinational enterprises also has a cross-cultural dimension — expectations around &quot;how much AI should manage&quot; vary enormously across cultures</li>
</ul>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>

<blockquote><p>AI can replace 70% of management work. But the remaining 30% — <strong>making value judgments under uncertainty, empathizing in complex situations, building trust within a team</strong> — is precisely the most valuable 30%.</p></blockquote>

<p>This 30% of value will determine whether an organization merely &quot;uses AI&quot; in the AI era, or truly <strong>becomes stronger because of AI</strong>.</p>

